{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1779649320,"nonce":"5+ZkOyzGmqZ8G6WkLimIk1ayYb8=","title":"Subgraph editor can leak parent nodes across graph boundaries","message":"## Summary\nA user reproduced a serious Studio editor boundary bug: after dragging a Network composite into a graph that already had a top-level input_mux wired to task data, entering the Network subgraph showed an input_mux that appeared to be the parent/top-level node. Deleting that input_mux while inside the subgraph also removed it from the top-level graph after navigating back up.\n\n## Why this matters\nSubgraphs must be isolated graph layers. Parent components must not remain visible or editable inside child subgraphs unless they are explicitly represented as boundary ports/bindings. Deleting a node while editing a child graph must not delete a same-named node in the parent graph.\n\n## Observed reproduction\n1. Start from the RLRMP movement-ramp Studio graph with a top-level input_mux wired to task data.\n2. Drag a Network composite into the top-level graph.\n3. Enter the Network composite/subgraph.\n4. Observe an input_mux with task-data wiring visible inside the subgraph.\n5. Delete that input_mux while in the subgraph.\n6. Navigate back to the parent graph.\n7. The parent/top-level input_mux is gone too.\n\n## Current code evidence\n-  creates an internal node with id , so a child graph can legally have the same local node id as the parent.\n-  sets the active layer to  or a freshly created subgraph, and pushes the parent layer to .\n-  deletes selected node ids from the currently active . If the active graph is accidentally still the parent layer, or if React Flow/UI selection state is not isolated between same-named parent/child nodes, deleting  inside the apparent child context can delete the parent node.\n-  restores , , , and  independently, without validating that the active graph matches the breadcrumb/current subgraph layer.\n-  uses cached graphs and registry template graphs without an explicit defensive clone at the layer boundary.\n\n## Fix direction\n- Add graph-layer invariants for subgraph entry, exit, restore, delete, and imported templates.\n- Ensure entering a subgraph deep-clones the child graph/ui layer or otherwise guarantees parent/child object isolation.\n- Ensure React Flow remounts or otherwise namespaces state strongly enough that same local node ids in different graph layers cannot share selection/deletion state.\n- Validate/restitch restored snapshots so active / and  represent one coherent current layer.\n- Add regression tests where parent and child both contain ; deleting the child  must preserve the parent node and parent task bindings.\n\n## Related\nThis is distinct from 56dfd97, which concerns long-term nested execution semantics. This issue is about editor isolation/corruption across subgraph boundaries.","files":null}]}